;; The program facts seon.db writes (session D1's proto.db.facts.edn) (namespaces, vars, the example task and note) and the
;; recording transaction of each eval request, as entity schemas. Stored shapes; the Datahike
;; schema is derived from them (seon.schema.datahike/declarations).
{;; ---- the program, as the compiler knows it (seon.program; inv4's design) ----
 ;; A FORM is the unit of reproduction: main evaluates forms, write-back writes them. Each
 ;; definition (var, multimethod method, protocol extension, namespace) points to the form
 ;; that last made it; what a form defines, declares and references comes from Jolt's
 ;; analyzer (jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap*, or analysis-only for a file), never from text.
 :form/id [:and {:seon.db/identity true :seon.db/content-id true :description "The form's identity: a content id, the unit of reproduction that a definition points to."} :string]
 ;; the exact text as written, and the whitespace and comments before it in its file
 :form/src [:string {:description "The exact text of the form as written, with the whitespace and comments before it in its file."}]
 ;; the digest of the form's namespace and its code: the form as the reader read it, printed, so with no
 ;; comment or layout (seon.program/form-ids), what a test's reach digest takes of the form;
 ;; written with the form, and a form without one (an older store's) is taken by its id
 :form/code [:string {:description "The digest of the form's namespace and its code as the reader read it, with no comment or layout."}]
 :form/lead [:string {:description "The whitespace and comments before the form in its file."}]
 ;; the form is the text of a source tree a generation was built from (seon.source's import):
 ;; the binary holds it compiled, so a process evaluates it again only where a changed macro
 ;; expanded or a changed schema reaches its contract (seon.program/load-changes!); a form an agent or a merge
 ;; makes never carries it. Its :form/order is its place in that file, so seon.program/load-order
 ;; keeps the order between two such forms (the file already loads)
 :form/source [:boolean {:description "True when the form is the text of a source tree that a generation was built from, which the binary holds compiled."}]
 :form/ns [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/ns-fact] :seon.db/index true :description "The namespace that the form belongs to."} :seon.db/ref]
 ;; its place among its namespace's forms: a sort key, not an index (seon.program/stable-places), so an inserted form moves no other; a new form after all others
 :form/order [:int {:description "The form's place among its namespace's forms, a sort key and not an index."}]
 :form/defines [:set {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/var-fact] :description "The vars that the form defines."} :seon.db/ref]
 :form/declares [:set {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/var-fact] :description "The vars that the form declares."} :seon.db/ref]
 ;; the vars it references, resolved by the compiler (program, library and clojure.core alike): callers are
 ;; one AVET seek
 :form/refs [:set {:seon.db/index true :description "The vars that the form references, resolved by the compiler."} :qualified-symbol]
 ;; the qualified names its quoted data holds (seon.program/quoted-names): names, not references,
 ;; so the reach never follows them; a caller listing reads them (seon.doc/callers: who names a
 ;; render, a proc, a form sent to an agent)
 :form/quoted [:set {:seon.db/index true :description "The qualified names that the form's quoted data holds: names, not references."} :qualified-symbol]
 ;; the ops of the requests it builds ({:op :done ...}: a map's constant keyword under :op), recorded
 ;; by the analyzer (seon.program/unit-account): a test that starts a root reaches the server's
 ;; answer of each op it sends, seon.server/op-answer's method, and no other (seon.program/reach-step)
 :form/ops [:set {:description "The ops of the requests that the form builds."} :keyword]
 ;; the qualified keywords its code and its contracts name, recorded by the analyzer from the read
 ;; it already does (seon.program/unit-account): the attributes, config keys and schema entries it
 ;; operates on; a key's forms are one AVET seek (seon.program/schema-dependents, my.agent/callers)
 :form/keywords [:set {:seon.db/index true :description "The qualified keywords that the form's code and contracts name."} :qualified-keyword]
 ;; the namespaces its require/use calls name
 :form/requires [:set {:description "The namespaces that the form's require and use calls name."} :symbol]
 ;; references to clojure.core/intern, alter-var-root, eval, load-string, and definitions
 ;; inside a function body: judged at merge (every definition happens once)
 :form/banned [:set {:description "The references the form makes to intern, alter-var-root, eval or load-string, which are judged at merge."} :qualified-symbol]
 :form/inline-defs [:set {:description "The definitions inside the form's function bodies, which are judged at merge."} :qualified-symbol]
 ;; its catch clauses as EDN {:class name ("*" for a catch-all)
 ;; :answers :constant|:rethrows|:expr}: the facts the guard detectors query
 :form/catches [:set {:description "The form's catch clauses as EDN, the facts that the guard detectors query."} :string]
 :ns/name [:and {:seon.db/identity true :seon.db/content-id true :description "The namespace's name."} :symbol]
 :ns/doc [:string {:description "The namespace's docstring."}]
 :ns/form [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/form-fact] :description "The form that last made this namespace."} :seon.db/ref]
 ;; the file it was imported from, relative to the source tree's root (seon.source)
 :ns/file [:string {:seon.db/index true :description "The file the namespace was imported from, relative to the source tree's root."}]
 ;; the text after its file's last form
 :ns/tail [:string {:description "The text after the last form of the namespace's file."}]
 :var/name [:and {:seon.db/identity true :seon.db/content-id true :description "The var's qualified name."} :symbol]
 :var/ns [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/ns-fact] :description "The namespace that the var belongs to."} :seon.db/ref]
 :var/form [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/form-fact] :description "The form that last made this var."} :seon.db/ref]
 :var/doc [:string {:description "The var's docstring."}]
 ;; the var's own Clojure metadata keys (location keys and :doc left out: the doc is :var/doc), recorded
 ;; by the analyzer (seon.program/def-meta): a test is a var whose meta holds :test, a macro :macro, a
 ;; function a defn made :arglists (docs/source.md#a-program-file). And its declared contract, as text
 :var/meta [:set {:seon.db/index true :description "The var's own Clojure metadata keys, with location keys and :doc left out. See docs/source.md#a-program-file."} :keyword]
 :var/contract [:string {:description "The var's declared contract, as text."}]
 ;; A TEST'S LAST RESULT, on its var entity on the branch whose agent ran it (seon.proofs/result-tx,
 ;; written by seon.agent/run-tests-forked when a run ends; task seon.proofs/test-results-are-a-few-facts,
 ;; R2a): overwritten in place, no history. The digest it was judged at; green, red, or flaky (green and red at
 ;; that digest, seon.proofs/verdict), or deferred (a process test the run left to root's gate), and present only while that digest is the test's reach digest now (a
 ;; moved digest retracts them: unknown); its failing assertions as data, the test child's reports (seon.proofs/failures,
 ;; every one; present only while not green, printed by the one printer where shown);
 ;; and running sums over the runs at this digest (a new digest resets them): mean = sum / runs, computed on read
 :seon.test/digest [:and {:seon.db/no-history? true} :string]
 :seon.test/status [:and {:seon.db/no-history? true :seon.db/index true} [:enum :green :red :flaky :deferred]]
 :seon.test/failures [:and {:seon.db/no-history? true} [:vector :seon.problems/assertion]]
 :seon.test/ticks [:and {:seon.db/no-history? true} :int]
 :seon.test/bytes [:and {:seon.db/no-history? true} :int]
 :method/key [:and {:seon.db/identity true :seon.db/content-id true} :string]
 :method/multi :qualified-symbol
 :method/dispatch :string
 :method/form [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/form-fact]} :seon.db/ref]
 :impl/key [:and {:seon.db/identity true :seon.db/content-id true} :string]
 :impl/protocol :qualified-symbol
 :impl/form [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/form-fact]} :seon.db/ref]
 ;; A SCHEMA ENTRY of a schema resource (resources/seon/schemas/*.edn) is program too
 ;; (seon.source; v1: the program is entities, schemas among them): its key, its form as
 ;; EDN text, the resource it is written back to, and whether the binary holds it (imported
 ;; from the tree a generation was built from; an entry an agent registers or a merge
 ;; carries never does). A FILE of the program (:file/path) holds its text as imported: for a resource of entries, the base write-back edits.
 :schema/key [:and {:seon.db/identity true :seon.db/content-id true :description "The key of a schema entry of a schema resource."} :qualified-keyword]
 :schema/form [:string {:description "The schema entry's form, as EDN text."}]
 :schema/file [:and {:seon.db/index true :description "The resource that the schema entry is written back to."} :string]
 :file/path [:and {:seon.db/identity true :seon.db/content-id true :description "A file of the program, by its path in the checkout (a script under bin/, the shipped config). It is changed on your branch as a definition is, (seon.repl/edit! path old new), and reaches disk after your merge. A file you write with the shell or spit is not on your branch and does not merge."} :string]
 :file/text [:string {:description "The text of the file as imported."}]
 :file/mode [:string {:description "The file's mode."}]
 ;; An ENTRY of the config file (docs/source.md#a-program-file): its key, its value's exact text with its tags unread, and its file. The config a process reads is built from these (seon.config/of-entries).
 :config/key [:and {:seon.db/identity true :seon.db/content-id true} :qualified-keyword]
 :config/form :string
 :config/file [:and {:seon.db/index true} :string]
 ;; what a function cost on this branch, one entity a function, sorted together by an issued id: docs/sharing.md#a-functions-cost-is-a-fact
 :seon.fn/var [:and {:seon.db/identity true} :qualified-symbol]
 :seon.fn/stats [:and {:seon.db/no-history? true} [:map [:calls :int] [:ticks :int] [:self-ticks :int] [:bytes :int] [:self-bytes :int] [:hist [:map-of :int :int]] [:worst [:map [:self-ticks :int] [:eval [:maybe :string]]]]]]
 :seon.db/fn-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true} [:seon.fn/var :seon.fn/var] [:seon.fn/stats {:optional true} :seon.fn/stats]]
 :seon.db/config-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true} [:config/key :config/key] [:config/form :config/form] [:config/file :config/file]]
 :seon.db/schema-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true} [:schema/key :schema/key] [:schema/form :schema/form]
                        ;; a schema registered at the REPL (my.schema/register!) has no file until a sync writes it to disk
                        [:schema/file {:optional true} :schema/file]]
 :seon.db/file-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true} [:file/path :file/path] [:file/text :file/text] [:file/mode {:optional true} :file/mode]]
 ;; A file's mode as git records it: executable or not, all a tree holds of it. Written by the import from the file on disk, committed by the write-back
 ;; (seon.source/modes). A script (:script on the allow-list, docs/source.md#a-program-file): its text is the file's, and what follows is derived from it, for reading.
 ;; its lead comment, what it's for
 :seon.script/says :string
 ;; the lines of its lead comment that name it, how to call it
 :seon.script/usage :string
 :seon.script/fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true :seon.render/hiccup seon.render.ui/script :seon.ctx/pattern [:file/path :file/mode :seon.script/says :seon.script/usage]}
                    [:seon.script/says {:optional true} :seon.script/says] [:seon.script/usage {:optional true} :seon.script/usage]]
 :seon.db/form-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true}
                      [:form/id :form/id] [:form/src :form/src] [:form/ns :form/ns] [:form/order :form/order]
                      [:form/code {:optional true} :form/code] [:form/lead {:optional true} :form/lead] [:form/defines {:optional true} :form/defines]
                      [:form/declares {:optional true} :form/declares] [:form/refs {:optional true} :form/refs] [:form/quoted {:optional true} :form/quoted] [:form/ops {:optional true} :form/ops] [:form/keywords {:optional true} :form/keywords]
                      [:form/requires {:optional true} :form/requires] [:form/banned {:optional true} :form/banned]
                      [:form/inline-defs {:optional true} :form/inline-defs] [:form/source {:optional true} :form/source]
                      [:form/catches {:optional true} :form/catches]]
 :seon.db/ns-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true} [:ns/name :ns/name] [:ns/doc {:optional true} :ns/doc]
                    [:ns/form {:optional true} :ns/form] [:ns/file {:optional true} :ns/file] [:ns/tail {:optional true} :ns/tail]]
 :seon.db/var-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true :seon.ctx/pattern [:var/name :var/ns :var/doc :var/meta :seon.test/status :seon.test/failures :seon.test/ticks :seon.test/bytes {:var/form [:form/id :form/src :form/defines :form/refs :form/quoted :form/keywords]}]}
                     [:var/name :var/name] [:var/ns {:optional true} :var/ns] [:var/form {:optional true} :var/form]
                     [:var/doc {:optional true} :var/doc]
                     [:var/meta {:optional true} :var/meta] [:var/contract {:optional true} :var/contract]
                     [:seon.test/digest {:optional true} :seon.test/digest] [:seon.test/status {:optional true} :seon.test/status]
                     [:seon.test/failures {:optional true} :seon.test/failures] 
                     [:seon.test/ticks {:optional true} :seon.test/ticks]
                     [:seon.test/bytes {:optional true} :seon.test/bytes]]
 :seon.db/method-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true}
                        [:method/key :method/key] [:method/multi :method/multi] [:method/dispatch :method/dispatch]
                        [:method/form {:optional true} :method/form]]
 :seon.db/impl-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true}
                      [:impl/key :impl/key] [:impl/protocol :impl/protocol]
                      [:impl/form {:optional true} :impl/form]]
 ;; ---- the example program's task and note ----
 :task/name [:and {:seon.db/identity true} :keyword]
 :task/plan :string
 :task/status [:and {:seon.db/index true} :keyword]
 :task/refs [:set :seon.db/ref]
 :note/text :string
 :note/agent [:and {:seon.db/index true} :keyword]
 :seon.db/example-task-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true}
                              [:task/name :task/name] [:task/plan :task/plan] [:task/status :task/status]
                              [:task/refs {:optional true} :task/refs]]
 :seon.db/note-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true}
                      [:note/text :note/text] [:note/agent :note/agent]]
 :seon.db/text [:string {:description "Prose a person or a model wrote, in Markdown: drawn as html for a person, read as its structure by the model. See docs/render.md#a-value-draws-itself." :seon.render/hiccup seon.render.ui/text}]
 ;; ---- the recording transaction of each eval request, and the agent's answered t.
 ;; Observations: data on the branch that recorded them, never carried by a merge ----
 ;; an eval: one per eval request (a model's tool call, a form the user or a session typed),
 ;; its id a digest (seon.repl/eval-id: a model's eval, of its call and its place in the reply; any other, of a fresh UUID); who evaluated it (the agent's id, "user", "outside", "context")
 :seon.eval/id [:and {:seon.db/identity true :description "The eval's identity: a digest for a model's eval, a fresh UUID for any other."} :string]
 :seon.eval/agent [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/agent-fact] :description "The agent whose record holds this eval."} :seon.db/ref]
 :seon.eval/by [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/agent-fact :seon.root/root-fact] :description "Who evaluated it: an agent, a user or the system."} :seon.db/ref]
 ;; opened by a reader of the agent's page: true once, never retracted; root writes it on main as the
 ;; agent's :seon.agent/opened (an eval's id names it only within its agent), its turn on its branch
 :seon.eval/opened [:boolean {:description "True once a reader of the agent's page has opened this eval; never retracted."}]
 ;; what is stored of an eval's answer (:seon.repl/answer) beside its result: its handle, cost, namespace and warnings, each as the
 ;; answer has them (seon.repl/record-details, seon.db/with-response); its value, error and output are its result's, and
 ;; seon.db/response-of answers the whole (docs/users.md#what-each-thing-is)
 :seon.eval/response [:and {:description "What is stored of the eval's answer beside its result: its handle, cost, namespace and warnings. See docs/users.md#what-each-thing-is."} :seon.repl/answer :seon.db/plain]
 ;; the eval's result, one entity per answer: evaluating one value twice points both evals at it. Evaluating again moves the ref
 ;; and the old result stays. Never pull its reverse: every eval with no value points at the same nil
 :seon.eval/result [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.result/result-fact] :description "The eval's result, one entity per answer; evaluating one value twice points both evals at it."} :seon.db/ref]
 ;; a result: the full hash (hasch's uuid, as text) of what an answer is, [value ex out err forms], is its identity and its content
 ;; id, so two sessions that produce one value produce one entity; the handle result/<8 hex> is its short form and no identity
 ;; (two values sharing eight digits stay two entities). Its value made plain (seon.contract/plainly), its error the one error
 ;; map, what its forms wrote to *out* and *err* as seon.print/code, and how many forms ran when there were several
 :seon.result/hash [:and {:seon.db/identity true :seon.db/content-id true :description "The full hash of what an answer is, which is the result's identity and its content id."} :string]
 :seon.result/value [:and {:description "The result's value, made plain."} :any]
 :seon.result/ex [:and {:description "The result's error, as the one error map."} :seon.error/base]
 :seon.result/out [:map {:description "What the forms of the result wrote to out, as printed code."} [:seon.print/code :string]]
 :seon.result/err [:map {:description "What the forms of the result wrote to err, as printed code."} [:seon.print/code :string]]
 :seon.result/forms [:int {:min 2 :description "How many forms ran, when there were several."}]
 :seon.result/result-fact [:map {:seon.db/attributes true}
                           [:seon.result/hash :seon.result/hash] [:seon.result/value {:optional true} :seon.result/value]
                           [:seon.result/ex {:optional true} :seon.result/ex] [:seon.result/out {:optional true} :seon.result/out]
                           [:seon.result/err {:optional true} :seon.result/err] [:seon.result/forms {:optional true} :seon.result/forms]]
 ;; an eval's details, each its own attribute, each only when present (seon.repl/record-details): the code; the
 ;; model's intent beside the call; the provider's call id (the reply's text and reasoning are its model call's,
 ;; :seon.model-call/reply, which an eval's turn number names); its run (until runs go); its 10 costliest contracted functions
 ;; ([{:var :calls :ms}], seon.profile); a value that names its render schema keeps that schema, and a value
 ;; that names its basis (@ctx, seon.ctx) the basis it is rendered from, in place of a copy (the page and the
 ;; model draw it from the branch at its t). The response is :seon.eval/response, whole, its value as plain data
 ;; (seon.contract/plainly) up to :seon.config.store/max-value-chars; never a text made for a reader (seon.print
 ;; prints it when it is sent or shown). The intent is kept as written, never parsed as it is recorded
 :seon.eval/code [:string {:description "The code that was evaluated."}]
 :seon.eval/intent [:string {:description "The model's intent for this eval, kept as it wrote it."}]
 :seon.eval/call-id [:string {:description "The provider's call id for the model call this eval came from."}]
 ;; the model call of the turn in force when it was written (seon.task/call-ref; absent on root's, on an agent's own turn loop and on a context read)
 :seon.eval/call [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.model-call/fact] :description "The model call of the turn in force when this eval was written; absent on root's evals, on an agent's own turn loop and on a context read."} :seon.db/ref]
 :seon.eval/fns [:vector {:description "The ten costliest contracted functions of the eval, each with its var, calls and ms."} :map]
 :seon.eval/schema [:qualified-keyword {:description "The render schema that the eval's value names, kept in place of a copy."}]
 :seon.eval/basis [:map {:description "The basis (branch and t) that the eval's value is rendered from, when the value names one."} [:t :int] [:branch :seon.db/branch]]
 ;; an agent: one entity per agent, named by its key (seon.lib/unique-id: the digest of a fresh UUID),
 ;; a content id so root's entity on main and the one its branch's records name are one; it refs the root that started it. What root
 ;; observes of it (status, pid, memory, the form running, its task's description) is recorded
 ;; on it when root's view of it changes (seon.task/agent-tx)
 :seon.agent/key [:and {:seon.db/identity true :seon.db/content-id true :description "The agent's identity: the digest of a fresh UUID, the same on main and on its branch."} :string]
 ;; an agent's id in root's process and its procs: the keyword whose name is its key; never stored
 :seon.agent/id [:keyword {:description "The agent's id in root's process and its procs: the keyword whose name is its key; never stored."}]
 :seon.agent/root [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.root/root-fact] :description "The root that started this agent."} :seon.db/ref]
 :seon.agent/branch [:keyword {:description "The branch this agent works on."}]
 ;; the task it works (the agent's record pulls it, docs/ctx.md#the-pattern) and the turns it may take: raised to extend the agent
 ;; (the agent is the unit, seon.agent/the-agent-is-the-unit-not-the-run; max-turns is written from its cut 2, until then work!'s
 ;; :max-turns bounds the loop)
 :seon.agent/task [:and {:seon.db/to [:seon.task/task-fact] :description "The task this agent works. See docs/ctx.md#the-pattern."} :seon.db/ref]
 ;; the turns it may take, agent-wide: its :seon.agent/turn against it. Set at spawn from the config its task's environment names
 ;; (seon.reconcile/desired-env); raised, and the task released again (my.root/run!), it goes on (docs/turn.md#the-cap)
 ;; at least one turn: the environment it is read into (:seon.task/env) takes a positive count, one definition of it
 :seon.agent/max-turns [:and {:description "The turns the agent may take, agent-wide; raised to extend the agent. See docs/turn.md#the-cap."} pos-int?]
 ;; the agent's turn number (docs/turn.md#the-turn-is-a-number): moved by the model turn that starts (seon.turn/act!) in the
 ;; transaction of its model call's first facts, agent-wide and never restarting across wakes; every record takes the number in force
 :seon.agent/turn [:int {:description "The agent's turn number, agent-wide and never restarting across wakes. See docs/turn.md#the-turn-is-a-number."}]
 :seon.agent/description :seon.db/text
 ;; the model it works for, when its task names none of root's; the client that attached it, when an outside agent's (seon.server/held)
 :seon.agent/model [:string {:description "The model the agent works for, when its task names none of root's."}]
 :seon.agent/holder [:string {:description "The client that attached this agent, when it is an outside agent's."}]
 ;; root's boot it was started in, and when (ms): the agent is live in a root when the boot is that root's and its task is :running
 :seon.agent/boot [:and {:seon.db/index true :description "The root boot the agent was started in; it is live in a root when the boot is that root's and its task is running."} :string]
 :seon.agent/started-ms [:int {:description "When the agent was started, in ms."}]
 :seon.agent/ended-ms [:int {:description "When the agent ended, in ms."}]
 ;; a test run that failed as a whole (its fork refused, a namespace did not load): the error as data (its type, message and
 ;; data keys), written on the agent's branch by its turn (seon.turn/observe) and retracted when a run answers;
 ;; present only while it is so (seon.ctx.status/status)
 :seon.agent/test-error [:and {:description "The error, as data, of a test run that failed as a whole; present only while it is so."} :seon.error/base]
 ;; how its work ended, as the agent writes it on its branch (seon.agent/write-end!), naming its turn: root reads it at judging
 ;; (seon.reconcile/pull-end), and an end whose turn is not past the wake's is none (:no-end)
 :seon.agent/outcome [:enum {:description "How the agent's work ended, as the agent writes it on its branch."} :green :red :waiting :cap :failed :merge-failed :error]
 :seon.agent/reason [:string {:description "Why the agent's work ended as its outcome says."}]
 :seon.agent/ended-turn [:int {:description "The turn that the agent's end names."}]
 ;; an entity's own pattern, stored with it from its first change; before that it reads its schema's (its role's). The
 ;; description is what an agent reads beside its pattern (seon.render.ui/pull-front): what it is, the warning, one form that adds
 ;; a join. What a role's pattern must keep is that role's :seon.ctx/required, {attribute what-it-is}, a join's attribute or a
 ;; plain one (docs/ctx.md#changing-it-swap)
 :seon.ctx/pattern [:and {:description "Your pull pattern: what your pull holds, in Datahike's pull syntax, one clause an attribute or a join ({attribute sub-pattern}). Your page and your next request are made from it, so changing it changes what you read. ADD to it, never replace it: a pattern that drops a join your role requires is refused, and one that drops anything else simply stops showing it to you. Its first clause names your role's pattern (:seon.db/agent-fact), never a copy of it, so what your role gains later reaches you. To add a join: (swap! ctx update :seon.ctx/pattern (fnil conj [:seon.db/agent-fact]) {:my.ns/_of [:my.ns/text]})"}
                    [:vector :any] :seon.db/plain]
 ;; an entity's own render, as :seon.ctx/pattern is its own pattern: what its page is drawn by in place of its schema's (seon.render/render-var-of; docs/render.md#an-entity-may-name-its-own-render)
 ;; its description is what an agent reads beside the clause in its pull view (seon.render.ui/pull-front): what a render is handed
 :seon.render/hiccup [:and {:description "Your own render: the function your page is drawn by in place of your schema's, by its symbol. A render is (f opts node), node the pulled entity, and answers hiccup. opts always holds :seon.render/index, the entity index; name :seon.db/db or :seon.ctx/ctx in its opts contract, [:and :seon.render/opts [:map [:seon.db/db :any]]], to be handed the db at your page's point or your whole pull (as @ctx gives it). It is not handed the page drawn so far; the page at the previous stored point is to come (an owner's ruling), and what the walk drew is (my.render/tree) after it. Try a render on one node at your REPL: (f {:seon.render/index (seon.agent/branch-index)} node). To watch a render you are changing, draw it on your own page: docs/ctx.md#adding-a-datum-and-its-render."} :symbol]
 ;; the doc sections its system message is, when it names its own (docs/render.md#the-models-context-a-cached-prefix-then-the-turn); with none, docs/prompt.md's
 :seon.agent/system [:set {:seon.db/to [:seon.doc/fact] :description "The doc sections that make up the agent's system message, when it names its own. See docs/render.md#the-models-context-a-cached-prefix-then-the-turn."} :seon.db/ref]
 ;; a person's name, a lookup ref, and its presence is what makes an agent a user: a location /user/<name> reads it (docs/users.md#what-each-thing-is)
 :seon.user/name [:and {:seon.db/identity true} :string]
 ;; the agents this one started, a user or an agent alike (docs/users.md#what-each-thing-is)
 :seon.agent/agents [:set {:seon.db/to [:seon.db/agent-fact] :description "The agents this one started. See docs/users.md#what-each-thing-is."} :seon.db/ref]
